harness/artifacts/logs/dependency-check.stdout.log
================================================================================

================================================================================
WHAT THIS FILE ESTABLISHES
================================================================================

  The stdout of harness/bin/run-dependency-check.sh for this run, verbatim, and
  the two facts about this tool that a reader who assumes rather than reads gets
  wrong: the JDK it runs under, and the analyzer flags it passes.

  It carries the invocation as it happened - one direct call, no arguments, no
  orchestrator - its exit code, its wall time, the seeded NVD feed as found, the
  baked flag set as read out of the runner, the reach the runner actually had,
  and what there was in scope for this tool to resolve. Every figure below is
  spliced from a capture of a command that ran; none is transcribed by hand and
  none is carried over from any earlier run's record.

  AAP 0.6.1 makes this file the AUTHORITY for the artifact's byte count:
  harness/artifacts/raw/dependency-check.json conforms to the number stated
  here, not the other way round.

================================================================================
WHY THIS FILE CARRIES TWO VERBATIM STREAMS
================================================================================

  The runner splits its stdout across two destinations, so 'the stdout stream'
  is two byte sequences rather than one, and this file reproduces both:

    - The runner's OWN stdout - the banner scope_begin prints, the four
      configuration lines at runner lines 42-45, and the trailer scope_finish
      prints - goes to the runner process's file descriptor 1. APPENDIX A.
    - The TOOL's stdout is redirected by the runner itself to
      $HARNESS_LOG_DIR/dependency-check.stdout.log, which is this file's own
      path (runner line 26, redirected at line 57). APPENDIX B.

  They cannot be captured into one file by a plain redirect: line 57 opens that
  path with '>', which TRUNCATES it mid-run, so a banner written to the same
  path first would be destroyed and the trailer would overwrite tool bytes. The
  provisioned harness treats them as two streams for exactly that reason, and
  provisioning's own artefacts show the same split: its
  verify-run/logs/dependency-check.stdout.log holds the tool's stdout while its
  provision-log/runner-dependency-check.console.log holds banner through
  trailer.

  Both are reproduced here unedited and unindented, each with its byte count and
  sha256, between BEGIN/END markers - so a reader can extract either block and
  recompute the digest rather than take the word 'verbatim' on trust.

