================================================================================
HOW TO READ THIS FILE
================================================================================

  Plain text, in execution order. A command is introduced by '$ ' and followed
  by what it produced. A verbatim stream sits between
  '----- BEGIN VERBATIM: ... -----' and '----- END VERBATIM: ... -----' markers,
  unindented, with its byte count and sha256 stated above it.

  A verbatim block can be checked rather than believed. Extract the lines
  between a block's 'STREAM BEGINS ON THE NEXT LINE' marker and its END marker,
  keep them byte for byte including any trailing space, append a final newline,
  and the sha256 equals the digest stated in the block's own header. Both blocks
  in this file were verified that way after it was written.

  Where a claim rests on a line of the runner, that line is quoted with its
  number in APPENDIX F rather than paraphrased. Where a figure comes from the
  artifact, it was read out of the JSON rather than out of any prose.

  This file's own byte size and sha256 are not stated inside it - a file cannot
  contain its own digest. They belong to the manifest of both artifact trees
  that oss-scan-results/run-record.md owns (AAP 0.6.1), computed from the file
  as committed.

================================================================================
METHOD - THE FOUR PRINCIPLES THIS FILE IS HELD TO
================================================================================

  1. VERIFICATION IS INDEPENDENT OF THE THING VERIFIED. The JDK is established
     three ways in PHASE 1: from the runner's own text, from the JDK binary
     measured directly, and from the JVM process that actually ran the scan,
     sampled through /proc while it was in flight. The feed timestamp in PHASE 2
     is read out of the artifact the tool wrote, not out of a record.

  2. REJECT RATHER THAN INFER. Nothing is added and nothing paraphrased. Where a
     figure could not be established, it is named as such rather than omitted.

  3. HALT AND REPORT OVER SILENT REPAIR. Two expectations were not met - the feed
     timestamp and the elapsed time. Neither was repaired, neither was hidden,
     and neither is a halt condition. No flag was added, removed or forced, no
     runner was edited, and the allowlist was neither widened nor narrowed.

  4. VERBATIM PRESERVATION. Both stdout streams are reproduced byte for byte with
     their digests, and the tool's own words about its reach are quoted from its
     stream rather than summarised.

